WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.350 --> 00:00:07.600
The French Empire was one of the most powerful
in human history.

00:00:07.600 --> 00:00:12.240
At its height, it controlled almost ten percent
of the world’s land area.

00:00:12.240 --> 00:00:17.150
From Southeast Asia to West Africa, the country
we today associate with baguettes and cinema

00:00:17.150 --> 00:00:19.350
was a dominant global power.

00:00:19.350 --> 00:00:21.900
And that was particularly true in Africa.

00:00:21.900 --> 00:00:27.240
France oversaw territory spanning 17 modern-day
nations, from Morocco to the Congo.

00:00:27.240 --> 00:00:31.880
Under the banner of a “civilizing mission,”
France oversaw brutal occupations that engorged

00:00:31.880 --> 00:00:36.649
the wealth of the Empire, and left a terrible
legacy for ordinary Africans – marked by

00:00:36.649 --> 00:00:38.800
slavery, extraction, and murder.

00:00:38.800 --> 00:00:45.269
And today, France’s empire in Africa is
STILL around – in a streamlined, more profitable,

00:00:45.269 --> 00:00:47.760
and even more exploitative form.

00:00:47.760 --> 00:00:49.579
Let me explain.

00:00:49.579 --> 00:00:54.280
In the aftermath of the Second World War,
there was a wave of decolonial movements sweeping

00:00:54.280 --> 00:00:56.049
across Africa and Asia.

00:00:56.049 --> 00:01:00.469
And the French – humiliated by their defeat
and occupation at the hands of the Nazis – were

00:01:00.469 --> 00:01:02.629
eager to maintain their empire.

00:01:02.629 --> 00:01:08.240
So they responded to revolts in Algeria, Indochina,
and Madagascar with brutal reprisals.

00:01:08.240 --> 00:01:11.490
Maintaining foreign holdings became increasingly
difficult.

00:01:11.490 --> 00:01:16.140
By 1960, France was forced to grant independence
to almost all its colonies.

00:01:16.140 --> 00:01:19.330
But something really important happened in
Africa.

00:01:19.330 --> 00:01:23.619
Basically, the leadership of France decided
to keep their empire in Western and Central

00:01:23.619 --> 00:01:26.820
Africa intact in everything but name.

00:01:26.820 --> 00:01:28.200
The plan was simple.

00:01:28.200 --> 00:01:32.200
When an African country gained its independence,
it was made to sign a so-called “cooperation

00:01:32.200 --> 00:01:37.150
agreement” with France, which would outline
the nature of their relations moving forward.

00:01:37.150 --> 00:01:41.479
In exchange for French foreign aid, African
countries were required to give France rights

00:01:41.479 --> 00:01:45.659
over natural resources, allow France to maintain
troops in their territory indefinitely, and

00:01:45.659 --> 00:01:51.590
– most importantly – keep these countries’
currencies linked to France’s currency,

00:01:51.590 --> 00:01:52.869
the franc.

00:01:52.869 --> 00:01:56.260
Instead of having their own currencies, they
were to use the “Franc of the Financial

00:01:56.260 --> 00:02:00.330
Community of Africa” – what is now called
the “CFA franc.”

00:02:00.330 --> 00:02:04.190
The French framed these “cooperation agreements”
as a choice, but they were also clear about

00:02:04.190 --> 00:02:05.190
the consequences of defiance.

00:02:05.190 --> 00:02:10.710
When the leader of Guinea, a socialist named
Sékou Touré, rejected the cooperation agreement

00:02:10.710 --> 00:02:15.170
with France – and declared that he preferred
to be “poor in freedom than rich in slavery”

00:02:15.170 --> 00:02:17.800
– the French government decided to make
an example of him.

00:02:17.800 --> 00:02:22.581
They cut all foreign aid to Guinea, and did
everything they could to destabilize the government.

00:02:22.581 --> 00:02:26.610
They launched a secret campaign to print fake
Guinean banknotes and flood the country with

00:02:26.610 --> 00:02:27.780
them.

00:02:27.780 --> 00:02:32.710
One French spy later bragged: “The operation
was a total success: Guinea’s economy, already

00:02:32.710 --> 00:02:35.360
very weak, never fully recovered.”

00:02:35.360 --> 00:02:38.620
The French said that these countries existed
within the so-called “French Community.”

00:02:38.620 --> 00:02:41.940
But the policy would come to be known more
widely as “Françafrique.”

00:02:41.940 --> 00:02:46.690
It was about having the former colonies in
a position that was maximally advantageous

00:02:46.690 --> 00:02:48.730
to French interests.

00:02:48.730 --> 00:02:52.730
Many of the first generation of postcolonial
leaders in former French colonies had essentially

00:02:52.730 --> 00:02:54.450
been installed by the French.

00:02:54.450 --> 00:02:58.750
They spoke French, had spent time in France,
and were well-integrated with French elites.

00:02:58.750 --> 00:03:04.190
Jacques Foccart, a French diplomat, oversaw
French relations with Africa for almost thirty

00:03:04.190 --> 00:03:08.730
years, and built a huge web of client relationships
with African leaders, using corruption and

00:03:08.730 --> 00:03:12.160
covert operations to make them loyal subordinates.

00:03:12.160 --> 00:03:15.290
When local political orders were threatened,
the French weren’t afraid to protect their

00:03:15.290 --> 00:03:22.180
hand-picked dictators: since 1960, France
has invaded Africa more than fifty times.

00:03:22.180 --> 00:03:24.820
Look at the Central African country of Gabon
as one example.

00:03:24.820 --> 00:03:28.701
Gabon is particularly important to France,
because it has a huge supply of oil and an

00:03:28.701 --> 00:03:30.870
even bigger supply of uranium.

00:03:30.870 --> 00:03:35.920
Among the African colonies, Gabon was historically
one of the very closest to France.

00:03:35.920 --> 00:03:41.650
In 1967, a man named Omar Bongo became Gabon’s
president, soon turning the country into a

00:03:41.650 --> 00:03:42.970
one-party dictatorship.

00:03:42.970 --> 00:03:45.100
And Bongo was intimate with France.

00:03:45.100 --> 00:03:48.860
He had been appointed after flying to Paris
for what was basically a job interview with

00:03:48.860 --> 00:03:50.040
the President.

00:03:50.040 --> 00:03:55.740
So, under Bongo, France and Gabon enjoyed
a relationship that benefited both sets of

00:03:55.740 --> 00:03:56.740
elites.

00:03:56.740 --> 00:04:01.440
Gabon’s oil was pumped by the French state-owned
oil company Elf, and its uranium went right

00:04:01.440 --> 00:04:03.750
into France’s arsenal of nuclear weapons.

00:04:03.750 --> 00:04:09.080
In return, France subsidized Gabon’s budget,
especially the parts that flowed into the

00:04:09.080 --> 00:04:11.660
pockets of Omar Bongo and his family.

00:04:11.660 --> 00:04:15.720
At one point, Bongo was worth over one hundred
and thirty million dollars.

00:04:15.720 --> 00:04:18.950
Gabon, meanwhile, remained poor and underdeveloped.

00:04:18.950 --> 00:04:23.310
Under Bongo, it had one of the highest infant
mortality rates in the world.

00:04:23.310 --> 00:04:28.340
Instead of investing in the Gabonese economy,
Bongo spent state funds on influencing French

00:04:28.340 --> 00:04:33.410
politics in his favor, bankrolling the campaigns
of several future French presidents.

00:04:33.410 --> 00:04:38.130
Even today, France keeps troops in the country
to support Gabon’s current ruler, who happens

00:04:38.130 --> 00:04:40.280
to be Omar Bongo’s son.

00:04:40.280 --> 00:04:43.260
And Gabon is actually one of the happier stories.

00:04:43.260 --> 00:04:47.760
Other countries, like the Central African
Republic, are today some of the poorest in

00:04:47.760 --> 00:04:51.970
the world – in part due to the legacies
of French-backed dictators like Jean-Bédel

00:04:51.970 --> 00:04:52.970
Bokassa.

00:04:52.970 --> 00:04:56.760
But there’s another aspect of French influence
that’s even more important.

00:04:56.760 --> 00:05:02.380
Probably the most central part of all is the
CFA franc – the last colonial currency still

00:05:02.380 --> 00:05:03.880
in widespread use.

00:05:03.880 --> 00:05:08.940
In practice, the countries that use the CFA
franc have virtually no monetary sovereignty:

00:05:08.940 --> 00:05:11.490
the value of their currency is linked to the
Euro.

00:05:11.490 --> 00:05:16.060
The poorest countries in the world have a
currency controlled by the richest countries.

00:05:16.060 --> 00:05:20.630
And so the imperatives of European countries
– fiscal discipline and fighting inflation

00:05:20.630 --> 00:05:26.120
– end up shaping the very different world
of Western and Central Africa.

00:05:26.120 --> 00:05:29.630
That leads to an overly tight approach to
credit, something that’s necessary in our

00:05:29.630 --> 00:05:31.930
current system for an economy to grow.

00:05:31.930 --> 00:05:37.810
It means that any appreciation in the Euro
makes exports from these countries less price-competitive.

00:05:37.810 --> 00:05:40.060
So just look at what happened in Senegal.

00:05:40.060 --> 00:05:45.840
When the Euro appreciated against the dollar
from 2000 to 2009, the value of the CFA franc

00:05:45.840 --> 00:05:46.840
got higher.

00:05:46.840 --> 00:05:51.030
But this made local Senegalese rice more expensive
than imported rice from Thailand.

00:05:51.030 --> 00:05:55.490
So Senegal – which was trying to build its
own domestic rice industry – instead saw

00:05:55.490 --> 00:05:58.620
Thai rice wipe out local rice farmers.

00:05:58.620 --> 00:06:03.740
So because domestic products are expensive,
this makes export-driven growth nearly impossible

00:06:03.740 --> 00:06:06.400
which is necessary to lead a country out of
poverty.

00:06:06.400 --> 00:06:10.870
That’s why most countries that use the CFA
franc have growth rates significantly lower

00:06:10.870 --> 00:06:12.550
than their neighbors.

00:06:12.550 --> 00:06:16.590
Because the CFA discourages the development
of domestic industry, many of these economies

00:06:16.590 --> 00:06:18.680
have actually been shrinking.

00:06:18.680 --> 00:06:23.840
The Ivory Coast, the largest CFA franc country,
has a real GDP per capita one-third lower

00:06:23.840 --> 00:06:25.790
than it had in 1978.

00:06:25.790 --> 00:06:30.480
Other CFA franc countries like Cameroon and
the Republic of the Congo reached their highest

00:06:30.480 --> 00:06:33.790
levels of real GDP per capita in the seventies
and eighties.

00:06:33.790 --> 00:06:37.570
And because economic growth is weaker, there
is more incentive for local elites in these

00:06:37.570 --> 00:06:42.750
countries – always tied closely to French
multinationals – to take as much money out

00:06:42.750 --> 00:06:44.419
as possible.

00:06:44.419 --> 00:06:48.580
Billions of dollars have flooded out of these
countries and into shell corporations or foreign

00:06:48.580 --> 00:06:49.910
bank accounts.

00:06:49.910 --> 00:06:53.490
Through massive corruption, these African
elites have joined the ranks of the global

00:06:53.490 --> 00:06:54.490
super-rich.

00:06:54.490 --> 00:07:02.070
In 2007, for example, Omar Bongo’s daughter-in-law
appeared on VH1 to buy an LA mansion for 25

00:07:02.070 --> 00:07:03.470
million dollars.

00:07:03.470 --> 00:07:06.320
And elites in France get a great deal too.

00:07:06.320 --> 00:07:10.560
They enjoy cheap access to natural resources
and kickbacks from shady business deals.

00:07:10.560 --> 00:07:14.919
A French billionaire named Vincent Bolloré
now owns most of the major ports across West

00:07:14.919 --> 00:07:15.919
Africa.

00:07:15.919 --> 00:07:19.740
In France, Bolloré is known as the quote
“King of Africa.”

00:07:19.740 --> 00:07:22.550
So French colonialism in Africa has never
really ended.

00:07:22.550 --> 00:07:28.639
This is why academics use the term “neocolonialism”:
even as the branding of “empire” has faded

00:07:28.639 --> 00:07:32.330
away, the structures of economic extraction
have only grown stronger.

00:07:32.330 --> 00:07:36.480
This “neocolonialism” is actually a much
more efficient form of exploitation than the

00:07:36.480 --> 00:07:38.360
colonialism of the past.

00:07:38.360 --> 00:07:42.690
The French no longer pretend to care about
building functional governments or improving

00:07:42.690 --> 00:07:44.040
local living standards.

00:07:44.040 --> 00:07:46.970
It’s pure extraction, all the time.

00:07:46.970 --> 00:07:51.590
So these African countries are not under-developed:
they are over-EXPLOITED.

00:07:51.590 --> 00:07:57.100
France is almost totally reliant on its influence
in Africa for its economic power.

00:07:57.100 --> 00:08:01.450
In the words of Italy’s foreign minister,
“If France didn’t have its African colonies,

00:08:01.450 --> 00:08:06.250
because that’s what they should be called,
it would be the fifteenth largest world economy.

00:08:06.250 --> 00:08:10.960
Instead it’s among the first, exactly because
of what it is doing in Africa.”

00:08:10.960 --> 00:08:12.260
And the French know this.

00:08:12.260 --> 00:08:17.090
François Mitterand – former president of
France – said it himself: “Without Africa,”

00:08:17.090 --> 00:08:20.180
he declared, “France will have no history
in the twenty-first century.”

00:08:20.180 --> 00:08:25.770
Today, of course, France faces competition
from China within Africa, and new challenges

00:08:25.770 --> 00:08:28.230
to the colonial symbolism of the CFA franc.

00:08:28.230 --> 00:08:32.169
There are plans to change the currency’s
name and abandon the most overt symbols of

00:08:32.169 --> 00:08:37.080
the colonial past, to make it look like the
system is more African than French.

00:08:37.080 --> 00:08:41.029
But France has no intention of actually abandoning
a system of extraction that has made it one

00:08:41.029 --> 00:08:42.939
of the richest nations on earth.

00:08:42.939 --> 00:08:48.009
The real question is whether the next generation
of Africans will allow it to continue.

00:08:48.009 --> 00:08:51.149
I’m Ismael Loutfi, for the Gravel Institute.

